  mcqs: [{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Meaning of Office","n":1,"q":"An office is best described as","o":["a place only for sales","a store for raw materials","the administrative centre where information is received, processed, recorded, stored and communicated","a place used only for manufacturing goods"],"a":2,"e":"The office is the nerve centre of an organisation.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Meaning of Office","n":2,"q":"According to George R. Terry, an office is a place where","o":["goods are manufactured","customers are entertained only","records are prepared, stored and communicated to support business operations","stock is traded"],"a":2,"e":"Terry's definition used in the Unit 1 slides.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Meaning of Office","n":3,"q":"The office is often called the","o":["heart of the factory","engine room of production","nerve centre of an organisation","backbone of the market"],"a":2,"e":"Almost every department depends upon it.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Meaning of Office","n":4,"q":"Which is NOT a characteristic of an office?","o":["Coordination centre","Information centre","Manufacturing centre","Record centre"],"a":2,"e":"Offices are administrative, information, record, communication, coordination, service and decision-support centres.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Meaning of Office","n":5,"q":"A modern office may also be a","o":["virtual or digital workplace","only a godown","only a factory shed","only a physical room"],"a":0,"e":"An office need not be a physical place.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Importance of Office","n":6,"q":"Which statement about the importance of office is correct?","o":["It only stores raw materials","It only handles sales","It is unnecessary in modern business","It facilitates communication and provides information to management"],"a":3,"e":"Other importance: maintains records, supports decisions, coordination, saves time, efficiency.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Management","n":7,"q":"Office management means","o":["planning, organising, directing and controlling office activities for efficient and economical work","only filing documents","only typing letters","only buying furniture"],"a":0,"e":"It ensures proper use of human resources, space, equipment, time, money and information.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Management","n":8,"q":"Which is an objective of office management?","o":["To remove records","To delay decisions","To ensure smooth office operations","To increase paperwork"],"a":2,"e":"Others: reduce costs, improve efficiency, provide accurate information, maintain records, improve communication.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Management","n":9,"q":"Office management is considered both an","o":["accident","art only","science only","art and a science"],"a":3,"e":"Art: skills, creativity, leadership. Science: principles, systematic process, measurable results.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Management","n":10,"q":"Resolving a conflict between two employees professionally shows office management as","o":["a rule","an art","a science","a machine"],"a":1,"e":"It requires emotional intelligence and communication.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Management","n":11,"q":"Preparing salary sheets using software shows office management as","o":["luck","a science","an art","a hobby"],"a":1,"e":"It uses systematic procedures.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Management","n":12,"q":"Motivating employees is an example of","o":["filing","art","science","automation"],"a":1,"e":"It depends on leadership skills.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Functions of Office","n":13,"q":"Which of the following is a BASIC function of an office?","o":["Collecting taxes","Producing goods in factory","Receiving, recording, processing, storing and supplying information","Advertising on TV"],"a":2,"e":"Basic functions deal with information handling.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Functions of Office","n":14,"q":"Converting raw data like 100 complaints into a classified report is","o":["destroying information","storing information","receiving information","processing information"],"a":3,"e":"Raw data becomes useful information.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Functions of Office","n":15,"q":"Which is an ADMINISTRATIVE function of the office?","o":["Planning and supervising office work","Receiving mail only","Printing money","Sweeping the floor"],"a":0,"e":"Administrative functions: planning, organising, staffing support, coordination, controlling, correspondence, record and meeting management.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Organisation","n":16,"q":"Office organisation means","o":["buying new computers only","the systematic arrangement of people, resources, duties and procedures to achieve objectives efficiently","keeping the office clean only","paying salaries only"],"a":1,"e":"It decides who does what and who reports to whom.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Organisation","n":17,"q":"Which principle states that an employee should receive instructions from only one superior?","o":["Unity of command","Division of work","Flexibility","Coordination"],"a":0,"e":"Unity of command avoids conflicting orders.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Organisation","n":18,"q":"Dividing work according to specialisation is the principle of","o":["unity of command","division of work","simplicity","flexibility"],"a":1,"e":"Example: HR handles recruitment while Finance manages salaries.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Organisation","n":19,"q":"'Authority and responsibility should go hand in hand' is a principle of","o":["office organisation","marketing mix","recruitment","financial leverage"],"a":0,"e":"Listed among the nine principles of office organisation.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Manager","n":20,"q":"An office manager is the link between","o":["customers and suppliers","banks and borrowers","management and office staff","buyers and sellers"],"a":2,"e":"He plans, organises, coordinates and controls office activities.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Manager","n":21,"q":"Which is a function of the office manager?","o":["Advertising on social media only","Staff supervision and record management","Manufacturing goods","Auditing the company's accounts independently"],"a":1,"e":"Others: planning, organising resources, procurement, discipline, safety and cost control.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Support Services","n":22,"q":"Reception and mail service are examples of","o":["marketing research","office support services","production activities","financial leverage"],"a":1,"e":"Support services help employees and managers perform main duties.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Modern Office","n":23,"q":"Which is a characteristic of a modern office?","o":["No communication tools","Extensive use of technology and digital communication","Only paper records","Dependence on typewriters only"],"a":1,"e":"Others: automation, flexible working, paper reduction, faster retrieval, data-based decisions.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Modern Office","n":24,"q":"Instead of manually entering attendance, a biometric system generating an attendance report is an example of","o":["manual filing","typing","automation","dictation"],"a":2,"e":"Routine tasks can be automated.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Paperless Office","n":25,"q":"A paperless office means","o":["no paper is ever used","offices without computers","documents are primarily created, stored and communicated electronically, with minimum dependence on paper","offices without employees"],"a":2,"e":"It does not necessarily mean zero paper.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Paperless Office","n":26,"q":"Which is an advantage of a paperless office?","o":["Cannot be shared","Higher paper cost","More storage space needed","Faster retrieval of records"],"a":3,"e":"Others: saves space, eco-friendly, easy sharing, remote access, better tracking.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Paperless Office","n":27,"q":"Which is a limitation of a paperless office?","o":["Easy sharing","Saves storage space","Cybersecurity risk","Faster retrieval"],"a":2,"e":"Other limits: technology dependence, initial investment, employee resistance, data privacy.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Virtual Office","n":28,"q":"A virtual office is one where employees","o":["work only on factory floor","work without being permanently located in a traditional physical office","do not use computers","work only in one cabin"],"a":1,"e":"They stay connected digitally from home, co-working spaces or other cities.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Virtual Office","n":29,"q":"Which is a disadvantage of a virtual office?","o":["Higher office rent","Reduced flexibility","Limited face-to-face interaction","Smaller talent pool"],"a":2,"e":"Others: isolation, supervision difficulty, security concerns, technology dependence.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Virtual Office","n":30,"q":"Which is an advantage of a virtual office?","o":["Higher infrastructure cost","Lower office rent and wider talent pool","Fixed working hours","More commuting"],"a":1,"e":"Virtual offices reduce costs and increase flexibility.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Virtual Office","n":31,"q":"GitLab is an example of an organisation that moved to an","o":["all-factory model","single-cabin office","cubicle only","all-remote workplace"],"a":3,"e":"It hired worldwide and used cloud collaboration tools.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Virtual Office","n":32,"q":"A hybrid office combines","o":["only paper and typewriters","only cubicles","physical office work and remote work","only virtual meetings"],"a":2,"e":"Example: 3 days office and 2 days home.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Front & Back Office","n":33,"q":"The front office is the department that","o":["never meets customers","only keeps accounts","directly interacts with customers, visitors and other outsiders","only runs factories"],"a":2,"e":"Examples: reception, customer service, help desk.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Front & Back Office","n":34,"q":"Accounts, HR, IT support and administration are examples of","o":["front office","back office","middle office","virtual office"],"a":1,"e":"The back office does not interact directly with customers.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Front & Back Office","n":35,"q":"The risk management and compliance teams are examples of the","o":["middle office","front office","private office","back office"],"a":0,"e":"The middle office is supervised by back office managers.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Front & Back Office","n":36,"q":"In a hotel, the receptionist who welcomes you belongs to the","o":["back office","front office","virtual office","sublet office"],"a":1,"e":"The accounts team processing your bill is the back office.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Front & Back Office","n":37,"q":"An electronic office (E-Office) mainly aims to","o":["stop communication","increase paperwork","reduce paperwork and speed up operations","avoid computers"],"a":2,"e":"Examples: ERP, email, cloud storage, digital record management.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Layout","n":38,"q":"Office layout means","o":["the colour of the office","the location of the head office city","the number of directors","the planned arrangement of departments, furniture, equipment and employees within the office"],"a":3,"e":"Its objective is efficiency, comfort, safety and economy.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Layout","n":39,"q":"Which is an objective of a good office layout?","o":["Maximum movement of employees","Smooth flow of work and maximum space utilisation","Reduced communication","Poor supervision"],"a":1,"e":"Others: easy supervision, comfort, safety, lower cost.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Layout","n":40,"q":"A layout where employees work together in a large area without permanent walls is the","o":["closed office layout","cubicle layout","open office layout","private office layout"],"a":2,"e":"Open layout encourages teamwork but lacks privacy.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Layout","n":41,"q":"A major disadvantage of the open office layout is","o":["noise and lack of privacy","high cost of cabins","poor communication","high rent only"],"a":0,"e":"Open offices are cost-effective but distracting.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Layout","n":42,"q":"Separate rooms or cabins giving privacy and confidentiality describe the","o":["cubicle layout","modular layout","closed (private) office layout","open office layout"],"a":2,"e":"Disadvantage: higher cost and limited communication.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Layout","n":43,"q":"Partitioned semi-private workstations are used in the","o":["private cabins","cubicle layout","virtual office","open layout"],"a":1,"e":"It balances privacy and collaboration.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Layout","n":44,"q":"A layout with movable furniture that can be rearranged to meet changing needs is the","o":["modular / flexible layout","traditional layout","cubicle layout","closed layout"],"a":0,"e":"Initial setup cost can be high.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Layout","n":45,"q":"A layout combining open spaces, private cabins and meeting rooms is the","o":["open layout","closed layout","hybrid layout","cubicle layout"],"a":2,"e":"It supports flexible and remote working.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Types of Offices","n":46,"q":"A bank or lawyer's office with separate cabins and formal atmosphere is a","o":["coworking office","sublet office","creative office","traditional office"],"a":3,"e":"Traditional offices have high privacy.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Types of Offices","n":47,"q":"An office with open seating and few walls such as in Google is a","o":["traditional office","creative office","executive suite","sublet office"],"a":1,"e":"Employees work together.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Types of Offices","n":48,"q":"An office where different companies work in the same space and rent only the desks they need is a","o":["contiguous office","coworking office","private office","enterprise suite"],"a":1,"e":"Shared facilities, pay for the desks used.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Types of Offices","n":49,"q":"A ready-to-move-in office with furniture, internet and phone already installed is an","o":["contiguous office","sublet office","executive suite","enterprise suite"],"a":2,"e":"Like a fully furnished hotel room.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Types of Offices","n":50,"q":"An office rented from another tenant, usually short-term and cheaper, is a","o":["contiguous office","sublet office","private office","creative office"],"a":1,"e":"Suitable for temporary businesses.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Accommodation","n":51,"q":"Which factor determines the size of an office building?","o":["Brand of computers","Car park size only","Nature of business, number of employees and future expansion","Colour of walls"],"a":2,"e":"Others: volume of work, budget, need for storage.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Accommodation","n":52,"q":"Fire extinguishers, emergency exits and first-aid boxes are","o":["filing methods","security measures","safety measures","layout types"],"a":2,"e":"Safety protects from accidents.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Accommodation","n":53,"q":"CCTV cameras, ID cards and biometric attendance are","o":["indexing methods","security measures","safety measures only","etiquette rules"],"a":1,"e":"Security protects against theft and unauthorised access.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Environment","n":54,"q":"Lighting, ventilation, temperature and noise are part of the","o":["technological environment only","social environment only","physical environment","psychological environment"],"a":2,"e":"Motivation and job satisfaction belong to the psychological environment.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Environment","n":55,"q":"Stress, motivation, respect and recognition belong to the","o":["physical environment","legal environment","technological environment","psychological environment"],"a":3,"e":"A good environment improves productivity and retention.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Systems","n":56,"q":"Step-by-step methods followed for performing office tasks are","o":["office procedures","office manuals only","office furniture","office layouts"],"a":0,"e":"Examples: leave approval, purchase and recruitment procedures.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Manual","n":57,"q":"A written document containing office policies, procedures, rules and guidelines is the","o":["office index","office layout","circular only","office manual"],"a":3,"e":"It is a reference guide for employees.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Manual","n":58,"q":"An office manual helps in","o":["employee training and standardisation of work","removing policies","avoiding discipline","increasing confusion"],"a":0,"e":"It also helps maintain discipline and policy compliance.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Workplace Ethics","n":59,"q":"Which is a principle of workplace ethics?","o":["Conflict of interest","Dishonesty","Gossip","Confidentiality"],"a":3,"e":"Principles: honesty, integrity, confidentiality, respect, professionalism, accountability, fairness, avoiding conflict of interest.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Workplace Ethics","n":60,"q":"An employee who rejects a supplier's gift offered in exchange for approving a purchase order is following","o":["marketing strategy","cost cutting","workplace ethics","automation"],"a":2,"e":"The ethical decision is to reject the improper benefit.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Machines","n":61,"q":"A machine used for securely destroying confidential documents is a","o":["scanner","shredder","laminator","projector"],"a":1,"e":"Shredders protect confidentiality.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Machines","n":62,"q":"A scanner is used to","o":["convert physical documents into digital form","destroy documents","bind documents","project slides"],"a":0,"e":"It supports digitisation.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Machines","n":63,"q":"A biometric device is used for","o":["attendance and access control","photocopying","lamination","binding"],"a":0,"e":"It records fingerprints or other biometrics.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Machines","n":64,"q":"Which is a factor to consider before purchasing office equipment?","o":["Colour of the vendor's logo only","Cost, quality, maintenance and warranty","Number of vendor's employees","Distance of vendor's home"],"a":1,"e":"Others: durability, energy consumption, compatibility, spare parts.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Filing","n":65,"q":"Filing is","o":["destroying old documents","the systematic arrangement and storage of documents so they can be easily traced and retrieved","typing letters","dictating notes"],"a":1,"e":"Filing = Arrange + Store + Protect + Retrieve.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Filing","n":66,"q":"Which is an objective of filing?","o":["Easy retrieval and safe custody","Delaying work","Destroying evidence","Increasing confusion"],"a":0,"e":"Others: preservation, security, reference, legal evidence.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Filing","n":67,"q":"Which is an essential of a good filing system?","o":["Simple, economical, flexible and safe","Complicated and costly","Inaccessible","Inflexible"],"a":0,"e":"It should also be accurate, compact and easy to retrieve.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Filing","n":68,"q":"In centralised filing, documents of all departments are kept in","o":["one central filing department","employees' homes","bank lockers","each department separately"],"a":0,"e":"It gives better control and avoids duplication but may cause delay.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Filing","n":69,"q":"A disadvantage of decentralised filing is","o":["duplication and different filing standards","department-specific control","quick access","better local access"],"a":0,"e":"Decentralised filing is quicker locally but costlier.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Filing","n":70,"q":"Arranging files in A-to-Z order is","o":["numerical filing","geographical filing","alphabetical filing","chronological filing"],"a":2,"e":"Simple, but common names may confuse.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Filing","n":71,"q":"Assigning each file a number is","o":["numerical filing","geographical filing","alphabetical filing","subject filing"],"a":0,"e":"Requires an index.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Filing","n":72,"q":"Arranging documents by place such as state or city is","o":["chronological filing","numerical filing","subject filing","geographical filing"],"a":3,"e":"Useful for sales, banks, insurance and logistics.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Filing","n":73,"q":"Arranging documents by date is","o":["geographical filing","alphabetical filing","chronological filing","subject filing"],"a":2,"e":"Useful for meeting records, correspondence and attendance.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Filing","n":74,"q":"A combination of letters and numbers such as HR-101 is","o":["geographical filing","alphanumeric filing","numerical filing","chronological filing"],"a":1,"e":"It provides finer classification.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Filing","n":75,"q":"Which is the first step in the filing procedure?","o":["Retrieval","Transfer","Retention","Order to file"],"a":3,"e":"Steps: order to file, preparation, sorting and filing, follow-up slip, retrieval, transfer, retention.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Filing","n":76,"q":"A reminder attached to a document that needs future action is a","o":["follow-up slip","agenda","index card","visitor pass"],"a":0,"e":"Examples: purchase orders, payment bills.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Indexing","n":77,"q":"Indexing is the process of","o":["typing minutes","destroying files","photocopying files","preparing a guide that shows where a document is filed"],"a":3,"e":"Filing stores the document; indexing helps you find it quickly.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Indexing","n":78,"q":"The relationship between filing and indexing is that they are","o":["unrelated","opposites","the same thing","complementary activities"],"a":3,"e":"Library: bookshelf = filing, catalogue = indexing.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Indexing","n":79,"q":"Information recorded in a bound register, e.g. a school admission register, is a","o":["card index","strip index","wheel index","page (bound book) index"],"a":3,"e":"Simple and inexpensive but difficult to insert new entries.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Indexing","n":80,"q":"Each record on a separate card stored vertically in trays is a","o":["loose / vertical card index","strip index","wheel index","page index"],"a":0,"e":"Easy to add or remove cards.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Indexing","n":81,"q":"Cards arranged so that important information remains visible without removing them is a","o":["visible card index","strip index","page index","wheel index"],"a":0,"e":"Examples: hotel reservation desk, doctor appointment cards.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Indexing","n":82,"q":"Information on removable strips in a holder, such as an office telephone directory, is a","o":["page index","strip index","computerised index","visible card index"],"a":1,"e":"Convenient for frequently changing information.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Indexing","n":83,"q":"Cards mounted on a rotating drum for quick access form a","o":["wheel (rotary) index","loose index","strip index","page index"],"a":0,"e":"Used for reception contact directories.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Record Management","n":84,"q":"Which concept is broader: filing, indexing or record management?","o":["Filing","Typing","Record management","Indexing"],"a":2,"e":"Filing and indexing are components of record management.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Record Management","n":85,"q":"Weeding of old records means","o":["indexing","photocopying","removing obsolete or unnecessary records from active files","adding new records"],"a":2,"e":"It saves storage space and reduces clutter.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Record Management","n":86,"q":"Digitisation is","o":["manual filing","converting physical documents into digital form","destroying paper records","typing letters"],"a":1,"e":"Examples: scanning, digital signatures, digital archives.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Etiquette","n":87,"q":"Office etiquette is","o":["a type of furniture","a tax","the code of conduct governing professional behaviour in the workplace","a filing system"],"a":2,"e":"It includes manners, communication, dressing, body language and teamwork.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Etiquette","n":88,"q":"Which is a DO of office etiquette?","o":["Take personal calls loudly","Reach office on time and reply promptly","Gossip about colleagues","Interrupt colleagues"],"a":1,"e":"Other do's: keep phones on silent, be a team player, respect privacy.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Etiquette","n":89,"q":"Which is a DON'T of office etiquette?","o":["Gossiping and office politics","Being punctual","Appreciating colleagues","Respecting privacy"],"a":0,"e":"Avoid offensive language, interruptions and unprepared meetings.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 1","t":"Office Correspondence","n":90,"q":"Communication within the organisation through memos, circulars and notices is","o":["internal correspondence","advertising","external correspondence","auditing"],"a":0,"e":"External: business letters, quotations, purchase orders, complaints, enquiries.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Secretary","n":91,"q":"A Secretary who assists one executive personally with diary and correspondence is a","o":["private secretary","company auditor","director","office / administrative secretary"],"a":0,"e":"An office / administrative secretary supports an entire department.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Secretary","n":92,"q":"Which is a quality of a good secretary?","o":["Disorganisation","Confidentiality and integrity","Lack of discipline","Carelessness"],"a":1,"e":"Others: communication, organisational ability, time management, interpersonal skills, IT proficiency.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Secretary","n":93,"q":"Safeguarding sensitive files, salary data and executive decisions shows the secretary's","o":["dress sense","typing speed","confidentiality and integrity","time management"],"a":2,"e":"Confidentiality builds employer trust.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Secretary","n":94,"q":"Which is a duty of a secretary?","o":["Manufacturing goods","Managing diary and appointments, correspondence and records","Setting wages","Auditing accounts"],"a":1,"e":"Six areas: correspondence, diary and appointments, records, meetings and minutes, reception, confidential support.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Business Letter","n":95,"q":"A business letter is a","o":["personal diary","oral order","casual chat","formal written communication to conduct official business"],"a":3,"e":"It creates a permanent, legally admissible record.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Business Letter","n":96,"q":"Which is a function of a business letter?","o":["Avoids documentation","Creates a permanent record and builds goodwill","Replaces all email","Reduces trust"],"a":1,"e":"It also conveys requests, orders, complaints and replies.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Business Letter","n":97,"q":"Which part comes right after the inside (receiver's) address in the standard format?","o":["Signature","Complimentary close","Subject line","Letterhead"],"a":2,"e":"Order: letterhead, reference and date, inside address, subject, salutation, body, complimentary close, signature.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Business Letter","n":98,"q":"'Dear Sir/Madam' is the","o":["signature","complimentary close","salutation","subject line"],"a":2,"e":"Salutation greets the receiver.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Business Letter","n":99,"q":"'Yours faithfully' is the","o":["subject","complimentary close","salutation","body"],"a":1,"e":"It ends the letter before the signature.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Notice","n":100,"q":"A notice is a","o":["oral talk","private letter","formal written announcement issued to inform a specific group about an event, meeting or decision","balance sheet"],"a":2,"e":"It has the heading NOTICE, organisation name, date, subject, body and signature.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Notice","n":101,"q":"Which element is NOT part of a notice?","o":["Date","Complimentary close such as 'Yours faithfully'","Signature and designation of the issuer","The word NOTICE as heading"],"a":1,"e":"A notice has the heading, name of organisation, date, subject, body and signature of the issuer.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Agenda","n":102,"q":"An agenda is","o":["a notice of strike","a list of items to be discussed in a meeting, circulated in advance","minutes of a meeting","a purchase order"],"a":1,"e":"The secretary collects items and sequences them by priority.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Agenda","n":103,"q":"'Any other matter with permission of the Chair' appears at the end of an","o":["agenda","memo","circular","invoice"],"a":0,"e":"It lets members raise additional items.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Memo","n":104,"q":"A memorandum is used for","o":["communication with customers only","brief internal communication within the same organisation","external advertising","legal summons"],"a":1,"e":"It is shorter and less formal than a letter.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Memo","n":105,"q":"Submission of monthly attendance reports to the Administrative Office is typically communicated through a","o":["business letter to customers","memorandum","invoice","advertisement"],"a":1,"e":"The sample memo in the notes is addressed to department heads.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Circular","n":106,"q":"A circular is","o":["a private message to one person","a letter from a customer","an invoice","the same message sent simultaneously to a large number of people"],"a":3,"e":"Used for holidays, office timings, new policies or price-list updates.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Circular","n":107,"q":"An announcement of revised office timings for all employees in all branches is best sent as a","o":["memo to one person","private email","circular","quotation"],"a":2,"e":"A circular is for mass communication.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Email","n":108,"q":"Which is part of good email etiquette?","o":["A clear, specific subject line and proofreading before sending","No subject line","Using Reply All always","Many topics in one mail"],"a":0,"e":"Also: professional greeting and sign-off, concise paragraphs, judicious CC/BCC.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Email","n":109,"q":"Email has largely replaced physical letters for","o":["printing","bulk freight","fast, documented, day-to-day business communication","legal court notices"],"a":2,"e":"It is electronic exchange of official messages.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Reception","n":110,"q":"The reception desk is the organisation's","o":["last point of contact","first point of contact","accounts office","storage room"],"a":1,"e":"The secretary must greet, verify and route every visitor with courtesy.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Reception","n":111,"q":"What should the receptionist do immediately after greeting a visitor?","o":["Verify the purpose of the visit","Ignore the visitor","Let the visitor enter any cabin","Ask for payment"],"a":0,"e":"Steps: greet, verify purpose, visitor register and pass, inform concerned officer, manage waiting, escort and follow-up.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Reception","n":112,"q":"A visitor register is used to","o":["record purchase orders","record entry details and issue a visitor pass","record salaries","record taxes"],"a":1,"e":"It also records exit time.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Telephone","n":113,"q":"Which is a DO of telephone etiquette?","o":["Answer within 2-3 rings and identify yourself","Answer after ten rings","Say a blunt 'Hello'","Eat while talking"],"a":0,"e":"Others: speak clearly, take accurate messages, confirm and close politely.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Telephone","n":114,"q":"Which is a DON'T of telephone etiquette?","o":["Speak at moderate pace","Give confidential information over the phone","Take accurate messages","Greet the caller"],"a":1,"e":"Also don't leave callers on hold without updates or argue.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Appointment","n":115,"q":"Appointment scheduling means","o":["ignoring requests","printing a calendar only","planning and confirming meeting times between an executive and visitors to avoid clashes","cancelling all meetings"],"a":2,"e":"Process: receive request, check diary, confirm date/time/venue, enter in calendar and send reminder.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Appointment","n":116,"q":"Which is a digital tool used for appointment scheduling?","o":["Typewriter","Google Calendar / Outlook Calendar","Rubber stamp","Carbon paper"],"a":1,"e":"Also MS Teams / Zoom scheduler and automated reminders.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Mail Handling","n":117,"q":"The mail received by an organisation from outside is called","o":["circular","inward mail (dak)","internal memo","outward mail"],"a":1,"e":"Inward mail is received; outward mail is dispatched.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Mail Handling","n":118,"q":"Which is the correct order of steps for inward mail?","o":["Receiving, sorting, opening and stamping, recording, distribution","Sorting, distribution, receiving, recording","Distribution, recording, receiving, sorting","Recording, receiving, distribution, sorting"],"a":0,"e":"The received date-stamp is affixed after opening (except confidential mail).","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Mail Handling","n":119,"q":"Which mail is generally NOT opened by the mail clerk?","o":["Ordinary mail","Circulars","Registered mail","Mail marked 'confidential'"],"a":3,"e":"Confidential mail goes unopened to the addressee.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Mail Handling","n":120,"q":"The register in which incoming mail is entered with serial number, sender and subject is the","o":["Inward Mail Register","Minutes Book","Outward Mail Register","Visitor Register"],"a":0,"e":"It also notes to whom the mail is marked.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Mail Handling","n":121,"q":"Which is the correct order of steps for outward mail?","o":["Approval, dispatch, enveloping, drafting","Drafting, approval, recording, enveloping, dispatch","Recording, dispatch, drafting, approval","Dispatch, drafting, approval, recording"],"a":1,"e":"Outward mail is signed by the competent authority before recording.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Office Communication","n":122,"q":"An internal telephone exchange linking all departments is","o":["Email","Intranet","Fax","Intercom / EPABX"],"a":3,"e":"Intranet portals carry circulars, HR forms and notices.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Office Communication","n":123,"q":"Zoom, MS Teams and Google Meet are examples of","o":["filing cabinets","typewriters","shredders","video conferencing tools"],"a":3,"e":"They support remote meetings.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Office Communication","n":124,"q":"Internal websites for circulars, HR forms and notices are called","o":["internet cafés","public blogs","intranet portals","fax machines"],"a":2,"e":"Intranet is private to the organisation.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Office Automation","n":125,"q":"Using software to perform routine office tasks automatically is","o":["office automation","manual processing","dictation","typing"],"a":0,"e":"Examples: online leave portal, biometric attendance, e-invoice.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Office Automation","n":126,"q":"An online leave portal replacing a paper leave application is an example of","o":["manual filing","a lockout","a strike","a paperless office practice"],"a":3,"e":"Digital forms reduce paper use.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Office Automation","n":127,"q":"Which is an advantage of using AI tools in offices?","o":["Removes all employees","Saves time by automating routine tasks","Increases manual errors","Stops communication"],"a":1,"e":"Assignment-2 asks for three advantages of AI tools in offices.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 2","t":"Office Automation","n":128,"q":"Which is a smart-office task that AI tools can assist with?","o":["Manufacturing steel","Farming","Scheduling, email writing and meeting summaries","Printing currency"],"a":2,"e":"AI supports communication, scheduling, document and data analysis.","g":1,"x":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Office Forms","n":129,"q":"An office form is","o":["a company logo","a bank cheque only","a letter written freely","a printed or digital document with blank spaces in a fixed format to collect specific information"],"a":3,"e":"It standardises information collection.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Office Forms","n":130,"q":"Which is an importance of office forms?","o":["Wastes paper deliberately","Reduces coordination","Increases errors","Saves time and ensures accuracy"],"a":3,"e":"Others: aid retrieval, serve as evidence, improve coordination, reduce communication cost.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Office Forms","n":131,"q":"A leave application form is an example of","o":["a tax return","an internal form","an invoice","an external form"],"a":1,"e":"Internal forms are used within the organisation.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Office Forms","n":132,"q":"A purchase order is an example of","o":["an external form","an internal form","a memo","a record form only"],"a":0,"e":"External forms are used with outside parties.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Office Forms","n":133,"q":"A stock ledger card is an example of a","o":["record form","report form","operating form","manifold form"],"a":0,"e":"Operating forms: attendance sheet. Report forms: sales report.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Office Forms","n":134,"q":"Forms with carbon or NCR copies for simultaneous distribution are","o":["internal forms","report forms","multiple-copy (manifold) forms","single-copy forms"],"a":2,"e":"Single-copy forms are used once with no duplicate.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Office Forms","n":135,"q":"Which is an objective of forms?","o":["To remove records","To confuse employees","To increase unnecessary paperwork","To standardise the procedure of recording data"],"a":3,"e":"Also act as an internal control and reduce cost of routine work.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Forms Design","n":136,"q":"Which is a principle of forms design?","o":["Very small space","Complexity","Random order of items","Simplicity and logical sequence"],"a":3,"e":"Others: clarity of headings, adequate space, standard size, provision for copies, economy, identification.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Forms Design","n":137,"q":"Giving each form a title and serial or code number follows the principle of","o":["adequate space","identification","economy","simplicity"],"a":1,"e":"It aids reference and filing.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Forms Design","n":138,"q":"Avoiding unnecessary items and keeping the form short follows the principle of","o":["economy","authorisation","adequate space","identification"],"a":0,"e":"A stationery request form need not ask marital status.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Forms Control","n":139,"q":"Forms control means","o":["hiding forms from users","printing as many forms as possible","stopping all forms","systematically designing, producing, distributing and reviewing forms to avoid duplication and waste"],"a":3,"e":"A forms control committee approves new or revised forms.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Forms Control","n":140,"q":"Which is a step in forms control?","o":["Avoiding approval","Printing duplicates","Numbering and indexing, periodic review and standardisation","Ignoring obsolete forms"],"a":2,"e":"Others: forms analysis, central approval, stock and cost control, version control.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Forms Control","n":141,"q":"Version control on a form helps to","o":["increase printing cost","identify the latest approved version and avoid use of old forms","hide forms","duplicate forms"],"a":1,"e":"Revision number and date are shown on the form.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Forms Preparation","n":142,"q":"What is the FIRST step in preparing a form?","o":["Choose paper size","Print and distribute","Get the draft approved","Identify the purpose the form must serve"],"a":3,"e":"Then decide information, layout, size and copies, approval, print and distribute.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Forms Preparation","n":143,"q":"A leave application form generally includes","o":["supplier invoices only","stock details only","tax rates","employee details, leave details and authorisation fields"],"a":3,"e":"Name, ID, dates, reason and manager/HR approval.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Record Management","n":144,"q":"Record management is","o":["only photocopying","only typing","only buying files","the systematic control of creation, maintenance, use, storage, retrieval and disposal of business records"],"a":3,"e":"It covers the record's entire life.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Record Management","n":145,"q":"A record is","o":["a blank register only","an empty form","any document, paper or electronic, created or received as evidence of a business activity","a rubber stamp"],"a":2,"e":"A completed form retained as evidence becomes a record.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Record Management","n":146,"q":"Which is the correct life cycle of a record?","o":["Retention, creation, disposal, use","Storage, disposal, creation","Disposal, creation, storage, use","Creation, active use, storage, retention, disposal / archiving"],"a":3,"e":"Record life cycle runs from creation to disposal or permanent archive.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Record Management","n":147,"q":"Which statement is correct about forms and records?","o":["Forms and records are identical","A form asks for information; a record preserves information","Records are always blank","A record asks for information; a form preserves it"],"a":1,"e":"Memory aid from the detailed notes.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Record Keeping","n":148,"q":"Which is a need / importance of record keeping?","o":["Hiding information","Legal evidence and statutory compliance","Avoiding audits","Increasing duplication"],"a":1,"e":"Others: aids decision-making, quick retrieval, protects information, organisational memory.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Record Keeping","n":149,"q":"Records serving as proof in audits and disputes shows the importance of","o":["storage space","typing","quick retrieval","legal evidence"],"a":3,"e":"A payment dispute can be resolved by checking the payment record.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Record Keeping","n":150,"q":"A signed contract kept in a physical archive with a scanned PDF in a document management system is","o":["no record keeping","hybrid record keeping","manual record keeping only","electronic record keeping only"],"a":1,"e":"Hybrid combines physical and digital records.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Record Keeping","n":151,"q":"Classification of records on the basis of importance includes","o":["red, blue and green records","A, B, C only","vital, important, useful and non-essential records","front and back only"],"a":2,"e":"Vital records are irreplaceable, e.g. title deeds.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Record Keeping","n":152,"q":"Irreplaceable records such as title deeds and legal agreements are","o":["useful records","non-essential records","inactive records only","vital records"],"a":3,"e":"They must be best protected.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Record Keeping","n":153,"q":"Records frequently referred to in current operations are","o":["active records","vital records only","non-essential records","inactive records"],"a":0,"e":"Inactive records are rarely used but retained for reference or legal reasons.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Record Preservation","n":154,"q":"Which is a method of preserving records?","o":["Retention schedule, proper storage and digital backup","Leaving records in the open","Never indexing","Sharing passwords"],"a":0,"e":"Others: microfilming/digitisation, periodic weeding, security and confidentiality.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Record Preservation","n":155,"q":"A retention schedule fixes","o":["how long each type of record must be kept before disposal","the colour of files","the salary of clerks","office timings"],"a":0,"e":"It guides lawful and efficient disposal.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Centralisation","n":156,"q":"Which is an advantage of centralised record keeping?","o":["Uniformity in filing and avoiding duplication","Department-specific control","Faster access for every department always","No risk of overload"],"a":0,"e":"Disadvantages: delay in access and the central unit may be overburdened.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Centralisation","n":157,"q":"Which is an advantage of decentralised record keeping?","o":["No duplication","Perfect uniformity","Lower overall cost","Quick access for the department's own needs"],"a":3,"e":"Disadvantages: duplication, lack of uniformity, higher cost.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Digital Records","n":158,"q":"OCR in digital record systems is used to","o":["photocopy","print forms","convert paper records into searchable digital text","destroy records"],"a":2,"e":"OCR = Optical Character Recognition.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Digital Records","n":159,"q":"Which is a digital record system?","o":["Rubber stamp","Document Management System (DMS)","Filing cabinet","Carbon paper"],"a":1,"e":"Others: cloud storage, databases / ERP systems.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 3","t":"Digital Records","n":160,"q":"Which is a challenge of digital record systems?","o":["No sharing","Slower retrieval","More physical space needed","Cybersecurity and data-privacy risks"],"a":3,"e":"Other challenges: cost of technology and need for staff training.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Company Secretary","n":161,"q":"A Company Secretary is classified under the Companies Act 2013 as","o":["a customer","a shareholder only","an outside auditor","Key Managerial Personnel (KMP)"],"a":3,"e":"A CS must be a member of the ICSI.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Company Secretary","n":162,"q":"ICSI stands for","o":["International Company Secretaries Institute","Institute of Commerce and Secretarial Information","Institute of Company Secretaries of India","Indian Council of Secretarial Industries"],"a":2,"e":"Professional qualification: ACS/FCS after the CS course.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Company Secretary","n":163,"q":"A whole-time Company Secretary is compulsory for listed companies and public companies with paid-up capital of","o":["Rs 10 crore or more","Rs 1 crore","Rs 50 lakh","Rs 1 lakh"],"a":0,"e":"A small private firm may use a practising CS when required.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Company Secretary","n":164,"q":"A Company Secretary is appointed by","o":["the Registrar only","the customers","the trade union","a resolution of the Board of Directors"],"a":3,"e":"The Board decides terms, salary and duties.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Company Secretary","n":165,"q":"Which is a function of a Company Secretary?","o":["Advertising","Auditing the accounts","Manufacturing","Maintaining minutes books and statutory registers and ensuring legal compliance"],"a":3,"e":"Others: conducting meetings, preparing documents, corporate governance, advisory, follow-up.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Company Secretary","n":166,"q":"Statutory duties of a secretary include","o":["recruiting factory workers","fixing prices","maintaining registers and filing returns with the ROC","selling products"],"a":2,"e":"Administrative duties: correspondence, records, share transfers, routine.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Company Secretary","n":167,"q":"The secretary is called a link between","o":["competitors","the Board, management, shareholders and authorities","customers and suppliers only","banks only"],"a":1,"e":"He is also an officer, an agent and an adviser of the company.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Company Secretary","n":168,"q":"The secretary reports to the","o":["auditor only","Board","competitors","customers"],"a":1,"e":"He works daily with every department.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Directors","n":169,"q":"A director is","o":["a person appointed to the Board to participate in management, decision-making and supervision","a customer","an employee at the lowest level","a competitor"],"a":0,"e":"Directors are elected by shareholders and the Board acts collectively.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Directors","n":170,"q":"The minimum number of directors in a public company is","o":["1","15","2","3"],"a":3,"e":"Private company 2; One Person Company 1; maximum 15.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Directors","n":171,"q":"The minimum number of directors in a private company is","o":["3","7","2","1"],"a":2,"e":"A One Person Company needs 1.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Directors","n":172,"q":"The maximum number of directors for a company (as in the notes) is","o":["50","7","15","21"],"a":2,"e":"At least one director must have stayed in India for 182 days or more.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Directors","n":173,"q":"Which is a responsibility of directors?","o":["Act in good faith, with due care and skill and avoid conflict of interest","Misuse position for personal gain","Hide interest in contracts","Ignore compliance"],"a":0,"e":"They must also attend Board meetings and disclose interest.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Chairman","n":174,"q":"The Chairman is the person who","o":["sells shares","audits the books","keeps the minutes book only","presides over meetings of the Board and guides its functioning"],"a":3,"e":"He ensures the meeting follows the agenda.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Chairman","n":175,"q":"If votes are tied, the Chairman may use his","o":["veto power","casting vote","second quorum","proxy"],"a":1,"e":"If the Articles allow.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Auditor","n":176,"q":"An auditor is","o":["the company secretary","an independent professional (Chartered Accountant) who examines the financial records and reports whether they show a true and fair view","the Chairman","a director"],"a":1,"e":"The report is given to shareholders.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Auditor","n":177,"q":"An auditor is appointed by","o":["the company secretary","employees","shareholders at the AGM","the Chairman only"],"a":2,"e":"Normally for 5 years.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Meetings","n":178,"q":"A meeting is","o":["a solo task","a notice","a gathering of two or more persons for a common purpose","an invoice"],"a":2,"e":"Meetings help decision making and provide a legal record through minutes.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Meetings","n":179,"q":"A Board meeting must be held at least","o":["every week","once in 10 years","4 times a year, with a gap not above 120 days","once in 5 years"],"a":2,"e":"As given in the notes.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Meetings","n":180,"q":"The Annual General Meeting (AGM) is held","o":["every 5 years","monthly","yearly, within 6 months of the financial year end","only when needed"],"a":2,"e":"It is attended by all members.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Meetings","n":181,"q":"An Extraordinary General Meeting (EGM) is","o":["held yearly","held only by the auditor","called for urgent business","a class meeting"],"a":2,"e":"All members attend.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Meetings","n":182,"q":"A Class Meeting is attended by","o":["auditors only","all employees","customers","one class of shareholders"],"a":3,"e":"Held to change the rights of that class.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Meetings","n":183,"q":"A committee meeting, e.g. audit, nomination or CSR, is attended by","o":["any visitor","selected directors / committee members","all customers","the public"],"a":1,"e":"It discusses specific matters.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Meetings","n":184,"q":"Before a meeting, the secretary","o":["disposes of records","files the returns","fixes date and venue, sends notice and agenda and prepares papers and attendance sheet","signs the minutes only"],"a":2,"e":"During: checks quorum, assists the Chairman, takes notes. After: drafts minutes, circulates decisions, follows up.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Notice","n":185,"q":"Notice for a board meeting must be given at least","o":["30 days before","21 clear days before","7 days before","1 day before"],"a":2,"e":"A general meeting needs 21 clear days.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Notice","n":186,"q":"Notice for a general meeting such as an AGM must be given at least","o":["5 days before","1 day before","21 clear days before","7 days before"],"a":2,"e":"It is sent to every member, director and auditor.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Notice","n":187,"q":"A defective notice can","o":["avoid all disputes","improve attendance","make the meeting invalid","have no effect"],"a":2,"e":"The meeting's decisions can be challenged in court.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Agenda","n":188,"q":"Which item usually appears at the end of an agenda?","o":["Call to order","Notice","Any other business with Chairman's permission","Confirm minutes of last meeting"],"a":2,"e":"Agenda includes routine items, special items and 'any other business'.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Quorum","n":189,"q":"Quorum means","o":["maximum attendance","the minimum attendance required to legally conduct a meeting","the number of votes against","the number of directors"],"a":1,"e":"Without quorum, business cannot be transacted.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Quorum","n":190,"q":"The quorum for a Board meeting is","o":["all directors","10 directors","5 members","1/3 of total directors or 2 directors, whichever is higher"],"a":3,"e":"A board of 9 directors needs a quorum of 3.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Quorum","n":191,"q":"The quorum for a general meeting of a private company is","o":["30 members","15 members","5 members","2 members personally present"],"a":3,"e":"Public company: 5 (up to 1000 members), 15 (1000-5000), 30 (above 5000).","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Quorum","n":192,"q":"The quorum for a general meeting of a public company with up to 1000 members is","o":["15 members","2 members","5 members","30 members"],"a":2,"e":"15 for 1000-5000; 30 for above 5000.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Voting","n":193,"q":"Votes counted according to the voting rights of members is a","o":["show of hands","poll","proxy","postal ballot"],"a":1,"e":"In show of hands members raise their hands.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Voting","n":194,"q":"A member voting without being physically present, through a representative, is voting by","o":["casting vote","show of hands","proxy","poll"],"a":2,"e":"E-voting and postal ballot are also allowed.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Motion & Resolution","n":195,"q":"A formal proposal made by a member during a meeting is a","o":["quorum","motion","resolution","minute"],"a":1,"e":"Example: 'I propose that the company open a new branch in Lucknow.'","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Motion & Resolution","n":196,"q":"A formal decision of the meeting after a motion is approved by required voting is a","o":["agenda","resolution","notice","motion"],"a":1,"e":"Resolutions are ordinary or special.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Motion & Resolution","n":197,"q":"An ordinary resolution is passed by","o":["unanimity only","the Chairman alone","75% of votes","a simple majority"],"a":3,"e":"A special resolution needs at least 75% votes in favour.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Motion & Resolution","n":198,"q":"A special resolution requires at least","o":["75% (3/4) of votes in favour","25%","50%","10%"],"a":0,"e":"As given in the notes.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Motion & Resolution","n":199,"q":"A resolution passed by papers sent to directors is a","o":["ordinary resolution","circulation resolution","poll resolution","special resolution"],"a":1,"e":"Board resolution is passed by directors at a Board meeting.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Minutes","n":200,"q":"Minutes are","o":["the official written record of what happened and was decided at a meeting","the quorum","the notice of the meeting","the agenda"],"a":0,"e":"They are the legal proof of every decision.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Minutes","n":201,"q":"Minutes that record only decisions and resolutions passed are","o":["notice","minutes of narration","minutes of resolution","agenda"],"a":2,"e":"Minutes of narration add a summary of the discussion.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Minutes","n":202,"q":"The minutes should be entered in the minutes book within","o":["30 days","5 years","1 year","7 days only"],"a":0,"e":"Pages are numbered and signed by the Chairman.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Minutes","n":203,"q":"Which rule applies to the minutes book?","o":["Erasing is allowed freely","No blank gaps and no erasing; corrections by signed note","Pages may be left blank","It may be destroyed after a year"],"a":1,"e":"The minutes book is preserved permanently as legal evidence.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Minutes","n":204,"q":"Minutes are signed by the","o":["customer","any visitor","auditor","Chairman"],"a":3,"e":"The Chairman signs and dates them.","g":1},{"u":"Unit 4","t":"Meeting Documentation","n":205,"q":"Which is the correct meeting documentation flow?","o":["Follow-up, notice, minutes, agenda","Agenda, minutes, notice, follow-up","Notice, agenda, attendance, minutes, follow-up","Minutes, notice, agenda, attendance"],"a":2,"e":"Remember: Notice > Agenda > Quorum > Voting > Resolution > Minutes.","g":1}]
